  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "El Niño is a climate pattern marked by warmer-than-average sea surface temperatures in the central and eastern Pacific Ocean, causing weakened trade winds and significant global weather shifts. Typically lasting 9–12 months and appearing every 3-5 years, it often brings wetter conditions to the US South, warmer winters to the North, and suppressed Atlantic hurricane activity. Key Effects of El Niño United States: Wetter, cooler conditions from California through the South and Atlantic coast. Northern U.S. and Canada typically experience warmer, drier winters. Global: Often brings heavy rainfall and flooding to the west coast of South America (Ecuador/Peru) and drier conditions to the western Pacific, including parts of Australia and Indonesia. Marine Life: The reduction in upwelling of cold, nutrient-rich water along the Pacific coast disrupts marine ecosystems and fishing industries. Hurricanes: Tends to reduce the number of Atlantic hurricanes due to increased wind shear, while potentially increasing Pacific activity. Prediction and Duration Timing: Develops when trade winds weaken, allowing warm water to move eastward. Duration: Lasts about 9 to 12 months, often peaking during the winter months. Prediction: Monitored by organizations like NOAA and NIWA through sea surface temperature monitoring, with increased likelihood of development often identified months in advance. Note: For the 2026 season, there is a strong possibility of an El Niño developing, potentially causing significant shifts in climate patterns later in the year.",
